{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And the fourth generation shall return here.</b> Rashi, of blessed memory, explained that from the time the Egyptian exile began until Israel inherited the land was four generations, and these are: Judah, Perez, Hezron, Caleb — for Caleb son of Hezron was among those who entered the land." "<b>And the Ramban,</b> of blessed memory, wrote that 'the fourth generation' refers to the Amorite completing his iniquity: for from the day of the decree He granted him length, for He visits iniquity upon the third and upon the fourth generations; for if they return, He would not devote them to destruction but they would become tributary servants, or they would clear out for them. End of quotation. And what it says, 'the iniquity of the Amorite,' is as if it said 'the Canaanites'; but it mentioned the Amorite because he is the mightiest among them — as it is said (Amos 2:9): ״אשר כגבה ארזים גבהו וחסן הוא כאלונים״." "<b>Here</b> — until now. Until his measure is filled. And Scripture should properly have said 'they shall come here,' but it said 'they shall return' because Isaac and Jacob and his children — who are their seed — had already settled there: for Isaac never left the land; Jacob likewise, concerning whom it is written (Genesis 37:1): ״וישב יעקב בארץ מגורי אביו״. Therefore it now says, 'and the fourth generation shall return here,' for since the fathers, who are the collective root, were from there, Scripture may say of the collective of Israel who descend from them, 'they shall return here.' Or we may say further: it alludes to the supremacy of the Holy Land, where the Gate of Heaven is — the heavenly Temple corresponding to the earthly Temple — and from there the souls enter and depart. Therefore it says, 'they shall return here,' that is to say: from there they went forth and there they shall return. And because of this, the righteous and the devout yearn to be gathered there, so that the soul may find a path and a way to ascend and return to its root, to the Elohim who gave it." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> 'the iniquity of the Amorite is not complete' — the iniquity of the saying that he said, 'Whereby shall I know that I shall inherit it?'"], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {1 "The phrase כי הוא פוקד עון על שלשים ועל רבעים paraphrases Exodus 34:7; the Amos quotation is from Amos 2:9, though R. Bahya cites no reference.", 2 "The closing phrase 'לאלהים אשר נתנה' echoes Ecclesiastes 12:7.", 3 "The Midrash reads לא שלם עון האמורי as a play on the same phrase, attributing to Abraham the iniquity of his question in Genesis 15:8."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:12:44.651042079Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}